          question: "What is the difference between an oxidising and a non-oxidising biocide for cooling water?",
          answer: "Oxidising biocides \u2014 chlorine, bromine, chlorine dioxide \u2014 kill microorganisms by chemically oxidising their cell membranes and internal proteins. They act rapidly, leave measurable residuals testable with ORP or DPD kits, and degrade quickly, reducing environmental discharge concerns. Chlorine loses efficacy sharply above pH 8.0 (bromine and chlorine dioxide are much less affected), and overfed oxidisers can accelerate corrosion of copper alloys. Non-oxidising biocides \u2014 quaternary ammonium compounds, isothiazolinones, glutaraldehyde \u2014 penetrate biofilm more effectively, work across a wider pH range, and are not consumed by ammonia the way chlorine is. In Oman's industrial cooling systems, a dual-biocide programme combining an oxidising biocide for bulk water control with a non-oxidising biocide for shock biofilm treatment gives the best protection. Global Chemicals LLC supplies both types and can help design a programme for oil, gas and industrial cooling systems in Oman."
