          answer: "Monoethanolamine (MEA, 2-aminoethanol, CAS 141-43-5) is a primary alkanolamine with two key industrial uses: acid gas removal (gas sweetening) and chemical intermediate production. As a gas sweetening solvent, MEA absorbs both H\u2082S and CO\u2082 from natural gas, refinery gas, and flue gas in absorption columns at 40\u201360\xB0C, and releases them at 120\u2013130\xB0C in the regenerator, producing a purified gas stream. MEA is the most reactive alkanolamine \u2014 it achieves very low residual H\u2082S and CO\u2082 concentrations (sub-ppm levels) in treated gas, making it suitable where deep acid gas removal is required. However, MEA has significant limitations compared to MDEA: high heat of regeneration (high energy cost), tendency to degrade irreversibly with COS and CS\u2082, higher corrosivity at elevated concentrations (usually 15\u201320%, about 30% with corrosion inhibitors, vs 40\u201350% for MDEA), and foaming tendency. In Oman, MDEA has largely replaced MEA for new gas sweetening units, but MEA remains in some existing units and in CO\u2082 capture applications. Global Chemicals LLC supplies MEA for Oman's gas processing and chemical sectors."
